.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt CERTCTL 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm certctl
.Nd "tool for managing trusted and untrusted TLS certificates"
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl lv
.Ic list
.Nm
.Op Fl lv
.Ic untrusted
.Nm
.Op Fl BnUv
.Op Fl D Ar destdir
.Op Fl M Ar metalog
.Ic rehash
.Nm
.Fl E
.Op Fl BnUv
.Op Fl C Ar directory
.Op Fl R Ar directory
.Op Fl b Ar file
.Op Fl T Ar file
.Op Fl X Ar file
.Op Fl D Ar destdir
.Op Fl M Ar metalog
.Ic rehash
.Nm
.Op Fl nv
.Ic untrust Ar
.Nm
.Op Fl nv
.Ic trust Ar
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility manages the list of TLS Certificate Authorities that are trusted by
applications that use OpenSSL.
.Pp
The following options are available:
.Bl -tag -width 4n
.It Fl B
Do not generate a bundle.
This option is only valid in conjunction with the
.Ic rehash
command.
.It Fl C Ar directory
Set the trusted certificate output directory.
Requires
.Fl E
and overrides
.Ev TRUSTDESTDIR .
.It Fl R Ar directory
Set the untrusted certificate output directory.
Requires
.Fl E
and overrides
.Ev UNTRUSTDESTDIR .
.It Fl b Ar file
Set the output bundle file.
Requires
.Fl E
and overrides
.Ev BUNDLE .
These three output paths are literal, must be nonempty, and are not prefixed by
.Fl D .
.It Fl D Ar destdir
Specify the DESTDIR (overriding values from the environment).
.It Fl d Ar distbase
Specify the DISTBASE (overriding values from the environment).
.It Fl E
Select certificate files explicitly.
Only files named by
.Fl T
and
.Fl X
are read as certificate inputs.
The default search directories,
.Ev TRUSTPATH ,
.Ev UNTRUSTPATH ,
and legacy blacklisted directory are not consulted.
With no file arguments, both certificate sets are empty.
This option is only valid with
.Ic rehash .
.It Fl T Ar file
Select a trusted certificate file.
May be repeated; each file may contain multiple PEM certificates.
Requires
.Fl E .
.It Fl X Ar file
Select an untrusted certificate file.
May be repeated and requires
.Fl E .
An untrusted certificate takes precedence if also selected as trusted.
.It Fl l
When listing installed (trusted or untrusted) certificates, show the
full path and distinguished name for each certificate.
.It Fl M Ar metalog
Specify the path of the METALOG file
.Po
default:
.Pa ${DESTDIR}/METALOG
.Pc .
Use
.Pa -
to write the metalog to standard output.
This option is only valid in conjunction with the
.Ic rehash
command.
.It Fl n
Dry-run mode.
Do not actually perform any actions except write the metalog.
Outputs are encoded in memory, so metalog entries report the byte sizes that
would be written.
The planned names and sizes are useful for construction validation, but the
metalog is not authenticated and does not authorize a trust policy.
.It Fl v
Verbose mode.
Print detailed information about each action taken.
.It Fl U
Unprivileged mode.
Do not attempt to set the ownership of created files.
This option is only valid in conjunction with the
.Fl M
option and the
.Ic rehash
command.
.El
.Pp
Primary command functions:
.Bl -tag -width untrusted
.It Ic list
List all currently trusted certificates.
.It Ic untrusted
List all currently untrusted certificates.
.It Ic rehash
Rebuild the list of trusted certificates by scanning all directories
in
.Ev TRUSTPATH
and all untrusted certificates in
.Ev UNTRUSTPATH .
A copy of each trusted certificate is placed in
.Ev TRUSTDESTDIR
and each untrusted certificate in
.Ev UNTRUSTDESTDIR .
In addition, a bundle containing the trusted certificates is placed in
.Ev BUNDLE .
.It Ic untrust
Add the specified file to the untrusted list.
Note that the next
.Ic rehash
will remove it unless a copy of it is also placed somewhere in a
directory included in
.Ev UNTRUSTPATH .
.It Ic trust
Add the specified file to the trusted list, unless it is already
untrusted.
Note that the next
.Ic rehash
will remove it unless a copy of it is also placed somewhere in a
directory included in
.Ev TRUSTPATH .
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width UNTRUSTDESTDIR
.It Ev DESTDIR
Absolute path to an alternate destination directory to operate on
instead of the file system root, e.g.
.Dq Li /tmp/install .
.It Ev DISTBASE
Additional path component to include when operating on certificate directories.
This must start with a slash, e.g.
.Dq Li /base .
.It Ev LOCALBASE
Location for local programs.
Defaults to the value of the user.localbase sysctl which is usually
.Pa /usr/local .
.It Ev TRUSTPATH
List of paths to search for trusted certificates.
Default:
.Pa ${DESTDIR}${DISTBASE}/usr/share/certs/trusted
.Pa ${DESTDIR}${LOCALBASE}/share/certs/trusted
.Pa ${DESTDIR}${LOCALBASE}/share/certs
.It Ev UNTRUSTPATH
List of paths to search for untrusted certificates.
Default:
.Pa ${DESTDIR}${DISTBASE}/usr/share/certs/untrusted
.Pa ${DESTDIR}${LOCALBASE}/share/certs/untrusted
.It Ev TRUSTDESTDIR
Destination directory for copies of trusted certificates.
Default:
.Pa ${DESTDIR}${DISTBASE}/etc/ssl/certs
.It Ev UNTRUSTDESTDIR
Destination directory for copies of untrusted certificates.
Default:
.Pa ${DESTDIR}${DISTBASE}/etc/ssl/untrusted
.It Ev BUNDLE
File name of bundle to produce.
Default:
.Pa ${DESTDIR}${DISTBASE}/etc/ssl/cert.pem
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Construct a store from explicitly selected package or site certificate files:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
certctl -E -T /inputs/root.pem -X /inputs/denied.pem -D /candidate rehash
.Ed
.Pp
The input paths are used as given; they are not prefixed by
.Fl D .
Add
.Fl nU
and
.Fl M Ar plan.mtree
to emit planned output metadata without changing the store.
Reusing the same inputs and output settings is required when constructing
the planned result.
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
Missing directories in the default search paths are optional.
Directories explicitly named in
.Ev TRUSTPATH
or
.Ev UNTRUSTPATH
must exist and be readable.
An empty variable selects no directories; empty components within a
colon-separated list are rejected.
The legacy
.Pa ${DESTDIR}${DISTBASE}/etc/ssl/blacklisted
directory is also searched when present, except with
.Fl E .
.Pp
Unreadable inputs, malformed certificate blocks, files containing no
certificates, non-regular input files, and directory traversal errors cause
a nonzero exit before installed certificate files are changed.
Normal OpenSSL PEM parsing permits explanatory text around certificates.
.Pp
Output write, close, removal, and metalog errors cause a nonzero exit.
Each certificate or bundle is written and closed before replacing its
destination, but the complete store is not updated as one atomic transaction.
A failure may leave earlier files updated; callers constructing a protected
generation must reject the candidate on any error.
Concurrent modification of source or destination directories is not supported.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr openssl 1
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
first appeared in
.Fx 12.2 .
